## Module 1 — Foundations: 3 Types of AI & Choosing One

**Objectives:** tell apart generative AI, ML, and rule-based systems · apply the 4-step selection method · explain transfer learning & the customisation ladder

**Three types of AI — not two.** Most people know generative AI vs. ML. The exam also tests rule-based systems: explicit if/then logic written by humans, no learning involved.

| | Generative AI | Traditional AI/ML | Rule-based |
|---|---|---|---|
| **What it does** | Creates new content | Classifies/predicts from data | Follows fixed human logic |
| **Output** | New content | Categories/probabilities | Same result every time |
| **Best for** | Writing, creative work | Forecasting, scoring at scale | Regulated, must-be-explainable |
| **Limitation** | Probabilistic, may be inaccurate | Needs labelled data | Can't adapt without reprogramming |

**The 4-step selection method:**

| Step | Ask yourself |
|---|---|
| 1. Define the problem | What task needs automating or augmenting? |
| 2. Assess complexity | Structured/rules-based, or open-ended/creative? |
| 3. Consider data sensitivity & scale | Confidential/regulated data? What volume? |
| 4. Apply the decision framework | Match task type to AI type |

**The customisation ladder** — climb only as far as you need: Prompt engineering → RAG/grounding → fine-tuning → train your own model.

**Transfer learning** is the principle underneath all of this: knowledge learned on one task carries over to new tasks — why adapting a foundation model is dramatically cheaper than training one from scratch.

| Pretrained (foundational) model | Fine-tuned model |
|---|---|
| Trained on the largest datasets — general-purpose, broad | Builds ON TOP of a pretrained model with added specific training |
| Fast to deploy, cost-effective, ready out of the box | Improves domain accuracy — but costs more time/data/maintenance |

> ⚠️ **Trade-offs to weigh at every step of the ladder:** more capable models cost more · faster models may reduce quality · customisation adds ongoing maintenance overhead. There's no free upgrade — every rung up the customisation ladder trades one thing for another.

**✅ Knowledge check**
- Q: Bank needs identical, fully auditable claims decisions. **A: Rule-based system**
- Q: Does fine-tuning change facts or behaviour? **A: Behaviour, style, format — not facts**
- Q: Name the 4 rungs of the customisation ladder in order. **A: Prompt engineering → RAG/grounding → fine-tuning → train your own model**
- Q: A team needs a model fast, for general tasks, at minimum cost. Pretrained or fine-tuned? **A: Pretrained — fine-tuning only when general accuracy isn't enough**

