## Module 2 — Cost, Risk & Business Value

**Objectives:** name the 4 cost layers & how ROI is judged · explain the 3 risks and their mitigations · distinguish automation from augmentation

| Cost layer | What it covers |
|---|---|
| Token economics | Input + output tokens; ~4 characters ≈ ¾ of a word |
| Infrastructure | Compute (GPU/TPU), storage, networking/API calls |
| TCO | API + fine-tuning + RAG infra + training + change management |
| ROI | Time saved, productivity gains, quality improvement — weighed against TCO |

**The 3 risks and their mitigations:**

| Risk | Looks like | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Fabrication (hallucination) | Cites a source that doesn't exist | Grounding |
| Reliability | Same prompt, different quality | Content filtering |
| Bias | Outputs favour/disadvantage a group | Human review |

**Automation vs. augmentation:** correct answers favour AI *assisting* humans over fully replacing them, especially in business-critical scenarios.

> 💡 **Don't focus on cost alone.** Always evaluate cost vs. business value together — choose the approach that balances both, not just the cheapest option.

**High-value use cases:** content at scale, customer support (24/7, no staffing constraints), document processing at volume.
**Avoid generative AI when:** guaranteed accuracy is required (medical/legal), it's simple classification (ML is cheaper), or it's a fixed-rule process (automation scripts win).

**✅ Knowledge check**
- Q: Which mitigation pairs with "outputs favour one group"? **A: Human review** (root cause: bias)
- Q: Why does the exam reject "always fully automate" for high-stakes work? **A: Augmentation (human-in-the-loop) is preferred**

