## Module 4 — Data, Security & the ML Lifecycle

**Objectives:** name the 5 data quality dimensions · apply the CIA triad & 4 AI threats · walk the ML lifecycle incl. overfitting/underfitting/drift

| Data quality dimension | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | Correct and error-free |
| Completeness | Fully populated, not missing |
| Consistency | Aligned definitions across sources |
| Timeliness | Up to date |
| Relevance | Task-appropriate |

**3 data types — know the difference:** structured (rows/columns, e.g. a spreadsheet or database), semi-structured (some organisation but not rigid, e.g. JSON, emails with metadata), unstructured (no predefined format, e.g. free text, images, audio). Unstructured data points to generative AI; structured/numerical data points to traditional ML.

**Representative datasets** reflect the real population the system serves — non-representative data introduces bias. AI performance is directly tied to data quality — spot when data, not the model, is the root cause.

**Secure AI — CIA triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) + 4 threats:**

| Threat | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Prompt injection | Malicious instructions embedded in inputs |
| Data leakage | Sensitive data unintentionally exposed in outputs |
| Model misuse | AI used outside its intended, safe purpose |
| Training data attacks | Attacker corrupts/poisons training data |

**Security spans the whole solution, not just the model:**

| Security layer | What to check for |
|---|---|
| Application security | User input, prompts, outputs — prompt injection, improper input handling, unsafe outputs |
| Data security | Protection at rest, in transit, and in use — especially for sensitive/regulated data |
| Authentication vs. Authorization | AuthN = verifies WHO you are · AuthZ = controls WHAT you can access — both via identity and role-based permissions |
| Compliance | Privacy laws, auditing, regulatory controls — required whenever the scenario references them |

**ML Lifecycle:**

| Stage | Detail |
|---|---|
| Data preparation | 60-80% of project time — #1 cause of ML project failure |
| Model training | Select algorithm, split data, tune hyperparameters |
| Evaluation | Overfitting (good on training, poor on new data) · underfitting (fails to capture patterns) · accuracy/precision/recall/F1 |
| Deployment | Model available for real-world use |
| Monitor & retrain | Model drift — performance degrades as real-world patterns change |

**✅ Knowledge check**
- Q: Share of ML project time on data prep, and why it matters? **A: 60-80% — top cause of project failure**
- Q: Model great on training data, poor on new data. **A: Overfitting**
- Q: Performance degrades over months as patterns shift. **A: Model drift → monitor and retrain**

