## Module 3 — Prompting, Grounding & RAG

**Objectives:** explain prompt engineering's business impact · explain grounding · walk RAG's 3 components + 3 patterns · compare RAG vs. fine-tuning

**Prompt engineering** — role/persona, clear instructions, examples (zero/one/few-shot), output format, step-by-step reasoning. Try this first: every token costs money, and better prompts mean fewer costly retries.

**Grounding** connects a model to trusted external data so responses reflect current, authoritative information. Required whenever a scenario involves organisational data, up-to-date info, or accuracy requirements.

RAG's 3 core components: an **embedding model**, a **vector/search index**, and a **language model**. If the wrong data is retrieved, the answer will be wrong even though the model behaves exactly as designed — suspect retrieval quality first.

| RAG pattern | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Basic RAG | Single retrieval, then generation |
| Multi-step/iterative RAG | Multiple retrieval/refinement passes |
| Agentic RAG | Tools/workflows dynamically select and retrieve |

**RAG vs. fine-tuning — the most-tested comparison:**

| RAG | Fine-tuning |
|---|---|
| Adds knowledge/facts at question time | Adjusts behaviour, style, format |
| Data changes often? Just re-index | New data needs another training run |
| Cheaper, faster, attributable (citations) | Expensive, curated data, no citations |

**✅ Knowledge check**
- Q: Legal needs answers from daily-changing contracts, cited, no retraining. **A: RAG**
- Q: Grounded chatbot gives wrong answers, model behaves normally. **A: Retrieval quality issue**
- Q: Name RAG's 3 core components. **A: Embedding model, vector/search index, language model**

