## Module 8 — Driving AI Adoption

**Objectives:** adoption team vs. AI council · the 6-phase rollout · 4 barrier categories & AI champions · licensing by adoption stage

**Adoption team vs. AI council — don't mix these up:** the **AI council** sets strategy and governance (the "what" and "why"). The **adoption team** drives actual day-to-day usage — translating strategy into real behaviour change. It's cross-functional and ongoing, never a one-time rollout. Low usage or confusion in a scenario signals a missing/weak adoption team, not a governance problem.

**The 6-phase rollout:**

| Phase | What happens |
|---|---|
| 1. Define strategy & metrics | Set goals and how success will be measured before rollout starts |
| 2. Secure data first | Audit permissions & sensitivity labels BEFORE wide rollout |
| 3. Pilot small group | Test with a limited, controlled group first |
| 4. Measure & feedback | Collect real usage data and user feedback from the pilot |
| 5. Refine & scale in waves | Adjust based on feedback, then roll out in stages — not all at once |
| 6. Monitor usage & outcomes | Ongoing tracking after rollout — adoption is never "done" |

**4 barrier categories:**

| Barrier category | Examples |
|---|---|
| People-related | Fear of job replacement, low confidence, lack of training |
| Process & integration | AI disrupts workflows, unclear guidance |
| Trust & risk | Privacy, accuracy, reliability, bias concerns |
| Leadership & alignment | No clear ownership, no executive sponsorship |

> 💡 **Resistance is the most common barrier.** Low usage = adoption failure, not capability failure. If uptake is poor, the fix is change management — never "buy a better model."

**AI champions** are respected practitioners and early adopters — explicitly NOT senior leaders or IT staff. They extend, but never replace, the adoption team or governance.

**Impacts of AI adoption — second-order effects to plan for as adoption grows:**

| Impact area | What changes |
|---|---|
| Data | Greater reliance on high-quality data; exposes existing gaps in governance and access |
| Security | Expanded risk surface — greater need for identity-based controls and data protection |
| Privacy | AI interacts with sensitive/regulated data in new ways |
| Cost | Consumption-based models scale with usage — require active cost governance |

**Azure AI/Foundry subscription models** (separate from Copilot licensing — see Module 5):

| Model | Best for |
|---|---|
| Pay-as-you-go | Usage-based, flexible — experimentation and variable demand |
| Prepaid / commitment tier | Committed usage, predictable cost — steady production workloads |

**Maturity signal:** organisations typically move from pay-as-you-go to prepaid as usage stabilises. Subscription models are part of cost governance, not just billing.

> 🚨 **Exam trap:** "We restricted/limited AI usage to control costs" = a GOVERNANCE FAILURE. Correct cost governance manages HOW spend scales (budgets, tiers, monitoring) — never WHETHER AI is used. Memory trick: *"Govern the spend, not the use."*

**✅ Knowledge check**
- Q: Copilot deployed, usage low, staff confused. **A: Adoption failure**
- Q: Who staffs an AI champions program? **A: Practitioners/early adopters, not IT or leaders**
- Q: Name the 6 phases of the adoption rollout, in order. **A: Define strategy & metrics → Secure data first → Pilot small group → Measure & feedback → Refine & scale in waves → Monitor usage & outcomes**
- Q: A workload has steady, predictable production usage. Which Azure AI subscription model fits? **A: Prepaid/commitment tier**

